\noindent$^{*}$\, Carried over at $n{=}200$; the ablations are specified at $n{=}100$
per difficulty and are therefore not sample-size-matched to the control.

\noindent The reading order for this comparison is fixed in advance, so that the
conclusion cannot be chosen after the numbers arrive. A configuration is judged on the \emph{pair}
(quality, cost), never on quality alone. Deleting an agent that leaves duplicate rate
and grounding within noise of the control while removing a substantial share of the
calls is evidence that the agent is not earning its cost. Deleting an agent that
degrades either measure materially is evidence that it is. An agent whose removal
\emph{improves} a metric is the most interesting outcome of all, and would indicate
that the agent is actively harmful in its present form.

\subsection{Agent-merging results}
\label{sec:res-merge-results}

Table~\ref{tab:merge} sets out the merging comparison. The two
merged configurations are implemented and verified in
\texttt{merged\_agents.py}, which reuses the control pipeline's prompts verbatim
so that the merge is the only difference, and which emits the same artifact the
two published harnesses already read.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Agent-merging variants, planned at $n{=}100$ per difficulty. Higher is
better except duplicate rate and calls.}
\label{tab:merge}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{Duplicate} $(\downarrow)$
 & \textbf{Grounding} & \textbf{Eff.\ accept} & \textbf{Calls/q} $(\downarrow)$ \\
\hline
\texttt{agentic} (control)$^{*}$ & 0.280 & 0.624 & 0.720 & \pend \\
\texttt{merge\_vr} & \pend & \pend & \pend & \pend \\
\texttt{merge\_pg} & \pend & \pend & \pend & \pend \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent$^{*}$\, As in Table~\ref{tab:loo}, carried over at $n{=}200$ and not
sample-size-matched. The two merged configurations are implemented and verified in
\texttt{merged\_agents.py} but have not been run, so their cells are left open.

\noindent Both merges are changes to \emph{packaging} rather than to behaviour, so
their cost can be bounded from the implementation before anything is run. Writing $g$
for a drafting call and $v$ for a validation call, a question accepted on attempt $k$
costs the control $k(g+v)$ calls, since every failed attempt is validated and then
redrafted. In \texttt{merge\_vr} the validator returns its rewrite in the same reply,
so the redraft disappears whenever the merged call supplies a usable rewrite, and the
cost falls toward $k+1$. That saving is an \emph{upper bound}, not a prediction: it is
realised only when the merged call returns a structurally valid rewrite \emph{and}
selects \texttt{regenerate}. When it selects \texttt{fetch\_context} or
\texttt{replan\_topic}, both of which require work outside the model, the loop
must redraft anyway and the cost reverts to that of the control. The rate at which
the merged call supplies a usable rewrite is therefore itself a quantity to be
measured, and it is the single number that determines whether this merge is
worthwhile.

\noindent\texttt{merge\_pg} is a different case, and its interest is not cost at all.
It removes exactly one planner call \emph{per batch}, which amortised over a hundred
questions is negligible. What it changes is the \emph{information available when a
topic is chosen}: the control planner sees the whole quiz and can balance topics
across it, whereas the merged generator sees only the topics already used and can do
no more than avoid repeating them. Whether a locally-planning generator repeats
itself more than a globally-planning one is the question this configuration exists to
answer, and given that duplicate rate is the metric on which the agentic pipeline
earns its advantage (Section~\ref{sec:res-discuss}), it is a direct test of where
that advantage originates.


\section{Summary}